note right of m_storage #FFFBEB
  **CRASH SAFETY — the ordering above is deliberate**
  The watermark at step 7 (main.py:188) runs only AFTER the
  whole `for batch in batches` loop (main.py:163-184) has
  completed without raising. Consequences, verified:
  · **DuckDB: IDEMPOTENT.** upsert is DELETE-by-Id then
    INSERT inside one transaction (storage.py:149-161) plus
    an intra-batch drop_duplicates(keep='last') (:133).
    Die after step 5 and before step 7 -> the watermark is
    unchanged, the next cycle re-runs the SAME query and
    re-upserts the SAME rows. It converges. CORRECT.
  · **Cost:** if the watermark was NULL, the replay repeats
    the ENTIRE Bulk full extract. There is no resumability,
    no partial-progress marker and no reuse of the completed
    Bulk job id.
  · **Parquet: NOT idempotent.** Every replay writes NEW
    files with a fresh uuid4().hex[:8] (storage.py:64-68);
    the crashed run's partial files stay forever.
  · **Salesforce DELETEs are never propagated** to DuckDB or
    LanceDB by any code path in this worker.
end note

note right of m_rag #DC2626
  **THE WATERMARK ADVANCES OVER A FAILED RAG INDEX**
  main.py:177-184 catches every exception from
  index_records, logs `event=rag_index_error`, and then
  main.py:188 sets the watermark ANYWAY. Those chunks are
  never retried — the records will not be re-fetched until
  their SystemModstamp changes again. LanceDB silently
  diverges from DuckDB.
  Aggravating factors:
  · No re-embedding guard at all: no content hash, no
    system_modstamp comparison, no chunk-level dedup, so any
    change to ANY field re-embeds every long-text chunk of
    that record on the GPU (rag_index.py:97-154).
  · `dim` is honoured only at table creation (:73-89);
    changing EMBED_MODEL makes table.add fail forever.
  · No retry, no backoff on the embed POST (:47-51) — a
    single 503 from a reloading vllm-embed aborts the batch.
  · timeout=300 s is PER 32-text batch (:41) with no overall
    deadline across an unbounded number of batches.
end note

note bottom of m_client #FFFBEB
  **UNBOUNDED POLL, NO RETRY, NO RATE-LIMIT REACTION**
  · The Bulk job poll is `while True` at 5 s with NO cap, NO
    wall-clock deadline and NO shutdown check
    (sf_client.py:208-220) — a job stuck in InProgress spins
    forever and blocks every other object, because run_cycle
    is a plain sequential loop (main.py:239-243).
  · The ONLY retry anywhere is a single 401 re-auth
    (:138-143), which replays a POST and can create a second
    Bulk query job for the same SOQL.
  · No 429 / Retry-After handling. check_api_limits (:50-75)
    only WARNS at >= 80 % of the daily API quota; it never
    throttles, sleeps or aborts.
  · csv.DictReader's default field limit is 131 072 chars —
    exactly the Salesforce Long Text Area maximum — and
    nothing raises it, so one full-size long-text value
    raises `_csv.Error` at :233 and aborts that object's
    entire extract.
  · resp.text for a 10 000-row CSV page is materialised, then
    copied into io.StringIO, then into dicts: ~3× peak RAM.
end note

note bottom of m_auth #FFFBEB
  **CLOCK SKEW AND TTL ARE BOTH UNHANDLED**
  · exp = wall-clock now + 180 s with NO iat, NO nbf, NO jti
    and no skew allowance (sf_auth.py:20,:32-38). On a DGX
    with drifting time the flow fails as an opaque
    `RuntimeError: ... HTTP 400` (:121-123) that names
    neither the clock nor the error_description.
  · The except at :106-109 swallows every JSON-parse failure
    into `error = ""`, so an HTML 502 from a proxy loses all
    diagnostic content — only the status code survives.
  · TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS = 25 min is a GUESS (:45-47): this
    grant returns no expires_in. It self-heals via the 401
    retry at the cost of an extra round trip.
  · The token POST has NO retry and NO backoff (:83,:98) —
    one transient 503 from login.salesforce.com raises and
    kills the whole cycle (caught only at main.py:292).
  · `assert` at :67 is control flow and is stripped by
    `python -O`, after which get_token can return (None, None).
end note

note bottom of m_chunk #FFFBEB
  **"TOKENS" ARE WHITESPACE WORDS**
  chunk_text splits on `text.split()` (chunking.py:30), so a
  minified JSON blob with no spaces is ONE token -> ONE chunk
  of arbitrary size -> one embedding request far past the
  model's context window. config.yaml deliberately indexes
  12 JSON-bearing fields as rag_fields (config.yaml:547,
  786-787, 794, 815-816, 827, 834, 841, 852) — exactly the
  pathological input class. Conversely, 800 whitespace words
  of indented JSON or Rich Text HTML is far more than 800
  model tokens. There is no cap on chunks per field and no
  cap on total text entering embed (rag_index.py:128).
end note

note bottom of m_main #FFFBEB
  **STARTUP, SHUTDOWN AND LIVENESS**
  · load_settings() and load_object_configs() run OUTSIDE the
    try (main.py:261-262 vs the `try:` at :281), so a
    non-numeric SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES or malformed config.yaml
    crashes the process at startup. There is NO `restart:` on
    the sync-worker compose service (docker-compose.yml:291-334)
    while every other service has one — the container simply
    stays dead.
  · No HEALTHCHECK in the Dockerfile (1-34) and none in compose.
  · flag.stop is read ONLY by _StopFlag.sleep (:48) and the
    outer while (:280). Neither run_cycle, sync_object, the Bulk
    poll loop nor the embedding loop checks it — a `docker stop`
    during a full extract waits out the grace period and SIGKILLs.
  · Interval drift: the sleep at :291 starts AFTER the cycle, so
    the real period is interval + cycle duration.
  · Silent degradation: `except Exception: return fields, rag_fields`
    (:81-82) and `except Exception: return []` (:210-211) log
    NOTHING at all.
end note

note bottom of m_config #FFFBEB
  **CONFIG DRIFT AND A READ-ONLY MOUNT**
  · config.yaml is read once at startup (main.py:262) and never
    re-read, so editing the live-mounted file does nothing until
    the container restarts.
  · The compose mount is `:ro` (docker-compose.yml:324), so
    `python -m syncworker.objects add ...` run INSIDE the
    container — which README.md:217-231 instructs — fails at
    objects.py:152. Only `list` works in-container.
  · objects.py `_live_describe` conflates EVERY non-200 (401,
    403 REQUEST_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, 429, 500) with "object not
    readable" (:284), and main:364 then writes a config with
    that object DELETED.
  · SYNC_MAX_FIELDS defaults to 80 (config.py:39) while
    objects.py caps an imported object at 60 (:168) — runtime
    adoption immediately widens it back toward 80.
  · Boolean parsing accepts anything not in ("0","false","no")
    as true, so SYNC_AUTO_FIELDS=off silently means TRUE (:38).
end note

note right of m_storage #FFFBEB
  **WAREHOUSE SHAPE**
  normalize_records casts every value to str|None
  (storage.py:38-56), so CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (:140) makes
  EVERY DuckDB column VARCHAR — dates and numbers arrive at the
  SQL-writing model as text. No PRIMARY KEY and no index is ever
  created, so `DELETE ... WHERE Id IN (...)` (:156) is a full
  table scan per batch. `_sync_meta` lives in schema `main`, so
  the orchestrator's introspection query
  (schema_cache.py:47-53) exposes the watermark table to the LLM
  as if it were a business table. The module defines `log` at
  :21 and never uses it: a Parquet or upsert failure emits no
  telemetry from this file at all.
end note

legend right
  |= colour |= layer |
  |<#2563EB>   | frontend / edge |
  |<#7C3AED>   | orchestrator / app logic |
  |<#16A34A>   | inference / GPU |
  |<#D97706>   | storage / data |
  |<#64748B>   | external systems |
  |<#DC2626>   | risk / trust boundary |
  ----
  **Notation**
  Dashed red rectangle = trust boundary (credential material crossing a shared volume).
  Red arrow = credential-bearing, externally-reachable or config-mutating hop.
  Thick amber arrows 1 -> 5 -> 7 = the crash-safety ordering:
  read watermark, land the data, THEN commit the watermark. Never the other way round.
  Dotted purple rectangle = Python package grouping, not a process boundary.
  Every arrow label is "what · protocol · sync|async · file:LINE".
  Amber notes = retry / timeout / unbounded resource / swallowed exception, each anchored to a read file:LINE.
  "blocking" is stated on every hop because this service has NO async code at all:
  one thread, one cycle at a time, one object at a time (main.py:239-243).
endlegend

